[
  {
    "_comment": "CAPABILITY suite for Snowflake bulk dest (create/append/replace/merge; schema evolution is the schema-evolution suites'), via `--type connectors/snowflake.json`. Functions in functions/caps_snowflake.py; input from dev datagen caps_input/cap_rows. append->cap_append (grows on re-run), replace->cap_replace (stays 3). Domains: dev (datagen), snowflake (dest). MERGE (if_table_exists=\"merge\", CDC markers i/u/d/l/t): the 12 batches of functions/_merge_plan.py, each a caps_input publisher caps_merge_<batch> (functions/caps_merge_batches.py) and a snowflake_out subscriber snowflake_merge_<batch> onto the batch's target keyed on cap_id, marker in op (no engine set: the default, iceberg, is the one a merge takes). Not auto-triggered: after the monitor, an actions stage triggers them in order (a non-detached tdk fn trigger, which waits for the merge) and after each reads the target from Snowflake itself (scripts/verify_merge.py, tagged check) and fails unless it holds exactly the rows the plan says. cap_merge: b1 creates it, b2 is an initial load over stale rows, b3 coalesces several changes per key, b4 truncates, b5 merges onto the empty table, b6 loads then truncates mid-batch (the last reset wins), b7 merges onto what it left, b8 is an initial load of an empty table (a bare l). cap_merge_from_load: load1 is an initial load as the first write, load2 merges onto what it created. cap_merge_from_truncate: trunc1 is a bare t as the first write (an empty table), trunc2 merges onto it. A load is only ever a batch's first row, as a CDC source emits it."
  },
  {
    "domain-manifest": {
      "domains": [
        {
          "name": "${xp:name(dev)}",
          "system": "dev"
        },
        {
          "name": "${xp:name(snowflake)}",
          "system": "snowflake"
        }
      ]
    }
  },
